                "publishedAt": null,
                "snippet": "Lockout/Tagout of Machinery Energy Sources\nStandard operating procedure for servicing and maintenance\n5. Lockout and tagout procedure\nFollow these steps in order. Do not skip a step, even for short jobs.\n- Prepare. Review the machine-specific energy sources in Table 1. Identify every energy source and every isolating device. Collect personal locks, tags and a group lockbox if more than one person will work on the machine.\n- Notify. Tell the Production Supervisor and every affected employee that the machine will be shut down and locked out, and why.\n- Shut down. Stop the machine with its normal stop control. Do not use the E-stop as the normal stop.\n- Isolate. Switch off or close every energy isolating device in Table 1: the main electrical disconnect, hydraulic and pneumatic supply valves and any other feed.\n- Apply locks and tags. Fit a personal lock and tag to each isolating device. Each authorized employee applies their own lock. For a crew, put the keys of the machine locks in a group lockbox and each member adds a personal lock to the box.\n- Release stored energy. Bleed hydraulic accumulators and pneumatic lines, discharge capacitors, lower or block any suspended load. On a hydraulic press, lower the ram to the bed or fit the rated ram safety props before anyone enters the die area.\n- Verify zero energy. Return the controls to OFF after each check. Confirm that the machine cannot start by trying to start it from its main control station (the try-out). Check that gauges r",
